{"id":974,"date":"2020-11-13T11:44:47","date_gmt":"2020-11-13T19:44:47","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=974"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:13:11","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:13:11","slug":"codrone-arduino-communication-and-pairing","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-communication-and-pairing\/","title":{"rendered":"Communication and Pairing"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba08c3f\" name=\"6a2215ba08c3f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How computers read code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Learning how to write code is basically like learning how to communicate with a computer in its own language. One of the things you need to understand is how a computer reads code that you wrote.<\/p>\n<p>When reading your code:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Computers only understand certain words<\/li>\n<li>Computers read from top to bottom<\/li>\n<li>Computers read one line at a time<\/li>\n<li>Computers are case sensitive<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__step-media\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;827&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba08d92\" name=\"6a2215ba08d92\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Computers only understand certain words<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">When you write programs, make sure that you use words that the computer knows! For example, if you want your CoDrone to fly upwards, you might want to write something like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void setup(){\r\n\r\n    Takeoff!!\r\n    Hover for 3 seconds!!\r\n    \r\n    Ok, now land.\r\n\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>One problem: your computer doesn&#8217;t know what <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Takeoff!!<\/code> and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Ok, now land.<\/code> means! Instead, you would need to write something like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup(){\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n  CoDrone.pair();\r\n  CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n  CoDrone.hover(3);\r\n  CoDrone.land();\r\n\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop(){\r\n  \r\n}<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone\u00a0<strong>l<\/strong>ibrary.<\/li>\n<li>Connect to a CoDrone\u00a0at a baud rate of 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Take off<\/li>\n<li>Hover for 3 seconds<\/li>\n<li>Land<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>If that made no sense to you, that&#8217;s OK! In the following lessons, you&#8217;ll learn what all of the different functions and commands mean, as well as how to use them.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba08dfa\" name=\"6a2215ba08dfa\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Computers read from top to bottom<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Just like how you might read books written in English, computers read code from top to bottom. In this program, the first line that the computer would read is <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code>, and the last line that the computer would read is <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.land();<\/code>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup(){\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n  CoDrone.pair();\r\n  CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n  CoDrone.hover(3);\r\n  CoDrone.land();\r\n\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop(){\r\n  \r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba08e4b\" name=\"6a2215ba08e4b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Computers read one line at a time<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Although computers read code much faster than we can (more on that below), they can only read one line at a time. This means that only one command can be executed at a time.<\/p>\n<p>Keep in mind what we just said: computers can read extremely fast &#8212; like hundreds of thousands of times per seconds fast. That speed would put The Flash to shame! Even though the program is being read at only one line at a time, it might feel like it&#8217;s happening all at once on your end because of the speed at which a computer can read a program.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba08eb0\" name=\"6a2215ba08eb0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Computers are case sensitive<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Capitalization matters! This <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Hello<\/code> would be read differently than this <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">hello<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>Again, don&#8217;t worry if this doesn&#8217;t make sense right now! We&#8217;ll be covering more in the next couple of lessons. Just like learning any new language, you will get more comfortable with practice.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba08f16\" name=\"6a2215ba08f16\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding the library<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The first thing you need to do at the beginning of every CoDrone program is include the CoDrone library:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;<\/pre>\n<p>Wait&#8230;what\u2019s a library again?<\/p>\n<p>A library is a saved set of functions, variables, and other resources that can be used in your code. For example, if you know that you always want your CoDrone to take off by throttling up slowly for 3 seconds, you can save time and memory by saving this as a function in a CoDrone library! Instead of telling your CoDrone to \u201cthrottle up slowly for 3 seconds\u201d every time, you can create a function, or use one that&#8217;s already in the<strong>\u00a0library<\/strong>, to tell your CoDrone \u201ctakeoff\u201d. Your CoDrone will then perform the &#8220;takeoff&#8221; function!<\/p>\n<p>Any code that starts with <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone._____<\/code>is a function in the CoDrone library.<\/p>\n<p>Still not sure about libraries? Learn more on the\u00a0<a href=\"https:\/\/www.arduino.cc\/en\/Reference\/Libraries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-link-type=\"LINK\">Arduino website<\/a>.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba08f8d\" name=\"6a2215ba08f8d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Baud rate<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Once you include the CoDrone<strong>\u00a0<\/strong>library at the top of your code, you can use all of the CoDrone functions in the rest of your program!<\/p>\n<p>The first function you need to use is <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.begin();<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code>, which will start the connection to the Bluetooth board. Inside of the parentheses is the baud rate, which is the bits per second that communication will travel. This line of code should be in <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">void setup()<\/code>, and the baud rate should be 115200 since the Bluetooth board uses that rate for serial communication.<\/p>\n<p>This is what your code should look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {            \r\n\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200);         \r\n\r\n}<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Fun fact: baud rate is named after\u00a0<a href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/%C3%89mile_Baudot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-link-type=\"LINK\">\u00c9mile Baudot,<\/a>\u00a0a French telecommunication pioneer!<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\"><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba09023\" name=\"6a2215ba09023\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Pairing code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The next thing you need to do is pair with your CoDrone! There are a few ways to do this which we&#8217;ll go over in another lesson, but for now, the easiest thing to do is use the function <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.pair()<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>This next part is important. Read it as many times as you need to!<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pair()<\/code> will just pair with whichever CoDrone you were last paired to. However, if this is your first time using the remote and you haven&#8217;t paired with a CoDrone with this particular remote, it will just pair with the first available CoDrone it finds, then lock in with that CoDrone. From that point on, it will always try to pair with that CoDrone. If you&#8217;d like to pair with a new drone, you can check out the next lesson: Unlocking a Remote-CoDrone Pair.<\/p>\n<p>Since computers read code from top to bottom, this function needs to be underneath your baud rate.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void setup() {            \r\n\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200);          \r\n  CoDrone.pair();\r\n\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p><strong>Student Tip:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This will not make your CoDrone fly! Sorry! This will just establish a Bluetooth connection between your CoDrone and your Bluetooth board.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<h3 class=\"activity2__tip-title\">Instructor Tip:<\/h3>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf activity2__rtf--sm\">\n<p>If you are setting this up in a classroom for the very first time, we\u00a0<em>highly recommend<\/em>\u00a0running through the pairing process in this lesson before class. This will lock your remotes in to specific CoDrones so that each remote will always pair with a specific CoDrone. Here&#8217;s what we recommend as prep before the class:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Set the remote to upload mode by flipping DIP switch 1 up.<\/li>\n<li>Upload the final code in this lesson that specifically uses\u00a0<code class=\"code-snippet-inline\">pair()<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>Set the remote in run mode by flipping DIP switch 1 back down.<\/li>\n<li>Pair with the desired CoDrone to lock that remote in to that CoDrone. Make sure all other remotes are off!<\/li>\n<li>Label the remote and CoDrone with an identifying number.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Make sure to do this one at a time<\/strong>. If you need to unlock the remote from a CoDrone and pair it to a new CoDrone, you can follow the steps in lesson Unlocking a Remote-CoDrone Pair.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba0907e\" name=\"6a2215ba0907e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]This is what your final code should look like:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt; \r\n\r\n\r\nvoid setup() \r\n{ \r\n\r\nCoDrone.begin(115200); \r\nCoDrone.pair();\r\n\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid loop() { \r\n\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215ba090e1\" name=\"6a2215ba090e1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Friends forever<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Try to pair with your CoDrone with the code you just wrote!\u00a0If you&#8217;ve successfully uploaded and run your code, you should hear a beep as it searches for the drone, and then a chime once it&#8217;s paired.\u00a0The green light on the back of the CoDrone flashes when it is\u00a0unpaired, and becomes a solid green when it is\u00a0paired.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;978&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Instructor Tip: <\/strong>Remember, if a remote has ever paired with another CoDrone using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pair()<\/code>(with no parameters), then it&#8217;s been locked in to that CoDrone. Running the final pairing code here will only pair with the CoDrone the remote has been locked in with. If it&#8217;s the very first time, then it will pair with the first CoDrone it finds, then lock in to that CoDrone. If you want to pair a remote with another CoDrone, you&#8217;ll need to run through the unlocking process, which is covered in Unlocking a Remote-CoDrone Pair.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 How computers read code [vc_column_text] Learning how to write code is basically like learning how to communicate with a computer in its own language. One of the&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-communication-and-pairing\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1751,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1793,1482,853,694,171],"lesson-tag":[2163,2135,2164,2165,2166,2107,2122],"class_list":["post-974","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-communication","lesson-tag-pairing","lesson-tag-programming","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/974","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/974\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2404,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/974\/revisions\/2404"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1751"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=974"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=974"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=974"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}